<score-partwise version="3.1">
  <!--
    Regression sample for slurs that start or end beside the fingerings of their note (PR 1830) and ran into what else was
    there, and for slurs that start at a note with an ornament. Extracts from isc's library (comment on PR 1830):
    Measures 1 and 2: C5 with an inverted mordent and fingering 32, slurred to the G5 at the end of measure 2 (Bach, Invention
    No. 1, m. 14-15): the slur started at the note, under the mordent, which stayed there, and rising steeply to the next
    notes, ran into it. An ornament goes outside a slur, like an accent, but the mordent would then be too far from the note:
    the slur goes past it (Behind Bars p. 122).
    Measure 3: D6 (fingering 5) slurred to C#6 (fingering 4), stems down (Chopin, Ballade No. 1, m. 111): the slur ended
    beside the 4, on the sharp.
    Measure 4: F#5 C#6 F#6 (fingering 4) slurred to B5 D#6 G#6 (fingerings 1 and 3) (Ballade No. 1, m. 108): the slur ended
    beside the 3 and 1, on the sharp of the G#6.
    Measure 5, in 12/8: A5 (fingering 5) slurred to Ab5 (Chopin, Nocturne op. 9 no. 2, m. 27): the slur started beside the 5,
    low, and ran into the flat of the Ab5. It starts over the 5 again.
    Measures 6 and 7, in two flats, with a system break between them: G4 slurred to Bb4 (fingering 2) (Ballade No. 1,
    m. 82-83): the slur's second part ended beside the 2, and ran from the start of the staff through the key signature.
    Measure 8: G5 with a trill slurred to F5 (after Mozart, Sonata K. 545, 1st movement, m. 15, where it is an F#5): the slur
    started at the note under the trill and ran into it. The trill goes outside the slur, which passes under it.
    Measures 9 and 10, on a new system (Bach, Invention No. 1, m. 17-18): the slur of measure 9 starts beside the fingering 4,
    and the one of measure 10 ends beside the fingering 3, as before: the tie into measure 10 is next to its start, past the
    fingering as well as beside it.
  -->
